WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.201 --> 00:00:01.678
- Oh no, what are they doing?

00:00:01.678 --> 00:00:02.941
Oh no.

00:00:02.941 --> 00:00:04.568
Oh my God!

00:00:04.568 --> 00:00:05.695
Oh no!

00:00:05.695 --> 00:00:07.525
Alright, this is a perfect example of

00:00:07.525 --> 00:00:10.946
sometimes the evidence
just speaks for itself.

00:00:10.946 --> 00:00:12.299
This is -

00:00:12.299 --> 00:00:13.421
this is awful

00:00:13.421 --> 00:00:14.450
and hilarious.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:17.533
(upbeat music plays)

00:00:19.104 --> 00:00:20.337
Hey Legal Eagles.

00:00:20.337 --> 00:00:24.267
D. James Stone here teaching
you how to think like a lawyer.

00:00:24.267 --> 00:00:27.430
Today we're gonna review
Better Call Saul episode one.

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:29.210
This is really exciting for me because

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:31.494
I loved Breaking Bad.

00:00:31.494 --> 00:00:34.658
And the character of Saul
Goodman was particularly good

00:00:34.658 --> 00:00:37.500
because he gave such good legal advice.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.235
Of course, it was legal
advice in the context

00:00:40.235 --> 00:00:43.262
of a drug dealing psychopath but still,

00:00:43.262 --> 00:00:45.519
good legal advice nonetheless.

00:00:45.519 --> 00:00:47.641
Be sure to like and
subscribe to make sure that

00:00:47.641 --> 00:00:50.582
you never miss out on
a future Lawyer Reacts.

00:00:50.582 --> 00:00:52.961
And of course be sure
to leave your comments

00:00:52.961 --> 00:00:55.144
in the form of an objection.

00:00:55.144 --> 00:00:57.856
I will either overrule or
sustain your objections

00:00:57.856 --> 00:00:59.702
based on their legal merit.

00:00:59.702 --> 00:01:01.494
And of course stay until the end

00:01:01.494 --> 00:01:03.784
when I give Better Call Saul episode one

00:01:03.784 --> 00:01:06.630
a grade for legal realism.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:08.950
So without further ado, let's dig right in

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:11.813
to Better Call Saul episode one.

00:01:11.813 --> 00:01:15.822
- [Jimmy] Your brain is
just not all there yet.

00:01:15.822 --> 00:01:18.066
If we were all held responsible

00:01:18.066 --> 00:01:20.604
for what we did when we were 19.

00:01:20.604 --> 00:01:22.122
(laughs)

00:01:22.122 --> 00:01:24.800
I remember what it was like being a kid.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:26.223
Think back.

00:01:26.223 --> 00:01:27.754
(laughs)

00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:30.452
It's all... Judge, what would you say?

00:01:30.452 --> 00:01:32.413
(door creaks open)
These boys.

00:01:32.413 --> 00:01:35.959
- God, that is exactly what
courtroom bathrooms looks like.

00:01:35.959 --> 00:01:38.470
They are just out of the last century,

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:41.996
they've got those weird
push-up soap dispensers.

00:01:41.996 --> 00:01:43.663
Just horribly dirty.

00:01:44.531 --> 00:01:47.342
This seems pretty accurate so far.

00:01:47.342 --> 00:01:49.730
- 19, I can't, I don't...

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:52.813
These three young men, just like you.

00:01:53.659 --> 00:01:54.909
Just like you.

00:01:57.706 --> 00:01:59.789
(sighs)

00:02:02.598 --> 00:02:03.431
- God.

00:02:03.431 --> 00:02:06.944
And it's so funny because
lawyers really do practice their

00:02:06.944 --> 00:02:09.117
opening and closing
statements in the bathroom

00:02:09.117 --> 00:02:10.734
before they go in to court.

00:02:10.734 --> 00:02:13.777
That is probably where you'll
find most trial attorneys

00:02:13.777 --> 00:02:16.112
when court is about to start.

00:02:16.112 --> 00:02:18.529
(doors open)

00:02:20.715 --> 00:02:24.258
Alright, gave himself a pep talk.

00:02:24.258 --> 00:02:26.069
- Oh to be 19 again.

00:02:26.069 --> 00:02:27.010
(laughs)

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:29.789
- Okay, you can never just storm in

00:02:29.789 --> 00:02:30.856
to the middle of the well,

00:02:30.856 --> 00:02:33.479
the area between counsel
table and the judge.

00:02:33.479 --> 00:02:35.980
It does seem like there is
a prior relationship here

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:38.262
between the judge and Saul Goodman,

00:02:38.262 --> 00:02:39.748
given that the judge sent the bailiff

00:02:39.748 --> 00:02:41.359
into the bathroom to get him.

00:02:41.359 --> 00:02:43.746
I'm guessing the judge
has interacted with Saul

00:02:43.746 --> 00:02:46.494
many times before, so he's
giving him some leeway

00:02:46.494 --> 00:02:48.480
but you can't just burst
in there like that.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.352
But it seems like he may have
prior permission to do that so

00:02:51.352 --> 00:02:52.943
we'll keep watching.

00:02:52.943 --> 00:02:55.742
- [Jimmy] (coughs) But if
you're being honest I mean,

00:02:55.742 --> 00:02:56.876
really honest,

00:02:56.876 --> 00:02:58.429
you'll recall that you also

00:02:58.429 --> 00:03:01.320
had an underdeveloped 19 year old brain.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.725
Me personally, (laughs)

00:03:03.725 --> 00:03:05.701
if I were held accountable

00:03:05.701 --> 00:03:09.484
for some of the stupid
decisions I made when I was 19,

00:03:09.484 --> 00:03:10.484
oh boy, wow.

00:03:11.716 --> 00:03:13.398
And I bet if we were in church right now

00:03:13.398 --> 00:03:15.692
I'd get a big Amen.

00:03:15.692 --> 00:03:16.716
- This is really good.

00:03:16.716 --> 00:03:18.163
What Saul Goodman is doing,

00:03:18.163 --> 00:03:20.874
is he's making himself seem personable.

00:03:20.874 --> 00:03:22.658
Your credibility is everything

00:03:22.658 --> 00:03:24.209
when you're in front of the jury.

00:03:24.209 --> 00:03:25.613
So you want to make it seem like

00:03:25.613 --> 00:03:27.129
you are a relatable person.

00:03:27.129 --> 00:03:29.771
You're just another member of the jury.

00:03:29.771 --> 00:03:32.813
So he's doing a great job
of seeming self-deprecating

00:03:32.813 --> 00:03:35.532
and forming an emotional
connection with his jury

00:03:35.532 --> 00:03:38.126
in this what looks like
a closing argument so,

00:03:38.126 --> 00:03:40.106
so far very good.

00:03:40.106 --> 00:03:42.889
- Which brings us to these three.

00:03:42.889 --> 00:03:45.389
Now, these three knuckleheads,

00:03:46.488 --> 00:03:48.898
and I'm sorry boys but
that's what you are.

00:03:48.898 --> 00:03:50.601
They did a dumb thing.

00:03:50.601 --> 00:03:52.388
- That's also really good.

00:03:52.388 --> 00:03:54.112
When you have bad facts,

00:03:54.112 --> 00:03:56.603
you need to get ahead of
them to front run them.

00:03:56.603 --> 00:03:59.745
And to show that you're
aware of those bad facts,

00:03:59.745 --> 00:04:01.476
but to downplay them and to show

00:04:01.476 --> 00:04:03.888
that they aren't dispositive to your case.

00:04:03.888 --> 00:04:04.969
So I like what he's done.

00:04:04.969 --> 00:04:07.509
He's actually making his
own client seem personable.

00:04:07.509 --> 00:04:08.477
They're young,

00:04:08.477 --> 00:04:10.473
they're knuckleheads as he put it.

00:04:10.473 --> 00:04:12.672
He is making them seem relatable

00:04:12.672 --> 00:04:15.500
but also showing that
they have made a mistake

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:18.167
and trying to get ahead
of whatever bad facts

00:04:18.167 --> 00:04:19.724
are against his case.

00:04:19.724 --> 00:04:21.336
So far pretty good.

00:04:21.336 --> 00:04:23.615
- Fact one: Nobody got hurt.

00:04:23.615 --> 00:04:25.168
Not a soul.

00:04:25.168 --> 00:04:27.275
Very important to keep that in mind.

00:04:27.275 --> 00:04:28.108
Fact two:

00:04:29.041 --> 00:04:32.364
Now the prosecution
keeps bending this term

00:04:32.364 --> 00:04:34.129
"Criminal Trespass".

00:04:34.129 --> 00:04:36.728
Mr. Spanazo, the property owner,

00:04:36.728 --> 00:04:40.149
admitted to us that he keeps
most portions of his business

00:04:40.149 --> 00:04:44.193
open to the public,
both day and night, so.

00:04:44.193 --> 00:04:45.360
"Trespassing?"

00:04:46.878 --> 00:04:48.105
Eh, that's a bit of a reach,

00:04:48.105 --> 00:04:51.026
don't you think Dave? (laughs)

00:04:51.026 --> 00:04:53.818
- Ah, man I really like this as well.

00:04:53.818 --> 00:04:56.695
Keep in mind that a
trial can go on for days,

00:04:56.695 --> 00:04:58.021
and sometimes weeks.

00:04:58.021 --> 00:05:00.816
So there are hundreds if
not thousands of facts,

00:05:00.816 --> 00:05:03.727
dozens of people testifying.

00:05:03.727 --> 00:05:06.656
A trial is a very complex thing.

00:05:06.656 --> 00:05:08.845
So what Saul Goodman has done here

00:05:08.845 --> 00:05:12.857
is reminded the jury of
two, and only two facts.

00:05:12.857 --> 00:05:15.265
Because a trial is really, really complex

00:05:15.265 --> 00:05:17.220
and he just wants them
to focus on two things

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:19.477
that are particularly good for his case.

00:05:19.477 --> 00:05:21.602
So he points out that no one got hurt,

00:05:21.602 --> 00:05:24.248
which may be important
from a legal perspective

00:05:24.248 --> 00:05:26.952
in that one of the
elements of whatever crime

00:05:26.952 --> 00:05:30.528
they're being charged with may
be triggered by bodily harm.

00:05:30.528 --> 00:05:33.983
But, more importantly, it
shows on an emotional level

00:05:33.983 --> 00:05:36.786
that there's no need for the
jury to punish these boys

00:05:36.786 --> 00:05:37.816
because no one was hurt,

00:05:37.816 --> 00:05:39.988
so it dovetails on the emotional.

00:05:39.988 --> 00:05:42.476
And then the second
thing that he focuses on

00:05:42.476 --> 00:05:45.314
is the legal requirement
for criminal trespass

00:05:45.314 --> 00:05:46.614
not being made.

00:05:46.614 --> 00:05:49.582
If the property owner gives consent to

00:05:49.582 --> 00:05:51.724
someone entering their property,

00:05:51.724 --> 00:05:55.538
then these boys can't be
guilty of the crime of

00:05:55.538 --> 00:05:57.106
criminal trespass.

00:05:57.106 --> 00:06:00.545
So he's focusing on the one
hand on the emotional trigger

00:06:00.545 --> 00:06:01.819
that no one was hurt,

00:06:01.819 --> 00:06:05.290
and also on the legal
trigger that the element

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:07.102
of criminal trespass isn't met.

00:06:07.102 --> 00:06:10.146
So out of all the things that
the jury learned in trial,

00:06:10.146 --> 00:06:12.266
he focuses on these two salient points,

00:06:12.266 --> 00:06:13.887
which are both good for his case.

00:06:13.887 --> 00:06:15.665
And I think that that
is a very good way of

00:06:15.665 --> 00:06:18.393
going about your closing argument.

00:06:18.393 --> 00:06:20.166
- Here's what I know,

00:06:20.166 --> 00:06:24.148
these three young men,
near honor students all--

00:06:24.148 --> 00:06:25.118
- "Near honor students"

00:06:25.118 --> 00:06:26.857
- Were feeling their
oats one Saturday night,

00:06:26.857 --> 00:06:27.940
and they just

00:06:29.553 --> 00:06:32.082
went a little bananas.

00:06:32.082 --> 00:06:33.867
I don't know, call me crazy

00:06:33.867 --> 00:06:35.526
but I don't think they
deserve to have their

00:06:35.526 --> 00:06:39.580
bright futures ruined
by a momentary, minute,

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:43.135
never-to-be-repeated lapse of judgment.

00:06:43.135 --> 00:06:46.006
- Another good emotional point

00:06:46.006 --> 00:06:47.196
- You're bigger than that.

00:06:47.196 --> 00:06:48.541
- (laughs) That's really good.

00:06:48.541 --> 00:06:51.091
There are two rhetorical
devices that Saul Goodman is

00:06:51.091 --> 00:06:52.483
using here to great effect.

00:06:52.483 --> 00:06:55.623
The first is that he is
referring to the dramaturgical,

00:06:55.623 --> 00:06:59.081
so he is putting the jury up on a stage,

00:06:59.081 --> 00:07:01.194
showing them that people are watching.

00:07:01.194 --> 00:07:04.540
And number two, he is ascribing the values

00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:06.885
that he wants to the jury.

00:07:06.885 --> 00:07:09.248
He's saying that they
are bigger than that,

00:07:09.248 --> 00:07:12.070
hoping that they will
rise to the attribute

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:14.525
that he is already ascribed to them.

00:07:14.525 --> 00:07:17.277
So those are two killer
persuasive techniques

00:07:17.277 --> 00:07:19.644
that Saul Goodman has used here.

00:07:19.644 --> 00:07:21.826
- (wheels squeaking)

00:07:21.826 --> 00:07:26.493
- (laughs) Oh God, what have they done?

00:07:28.476 --> 00:07:31.965
Oh no, he's just going to show a video.

00:07:31.965 --> 00:07:34.469
(laughs)

00:07:34.469 --> 00:07:37.969
- (tape loading into VCR)

00:07:40.731 --> 00:07:42.643
- So because this is his closing argument,

00:07:42.643 --> 00:07:44.864
all the evidence has already come in.

00:07:44.864 --> 00:07:47.667
So, the prosecutor doesn't
have to lay any foundation,

00:07:47.667 --> 00:07:49.681
all of that has already been established

00:07:49.681 --> 00:07:51.402
during the course of the trial,

00:07:51.402 --> 00:07:53.562
and the judge has already
approved or denied

00:07:53.562 --> 00:07:56.401
all of the exhibits,
including God only knows

00:07:56.401 --> 00:07:57.999
what's going to be on this video tape.

00:07:57.999 --> 00:08:00.088
- [Man] He's dead, they
suck it all out, dumb ass!

00:08:00.088 --> 00:08:02.167
- [Brunette Man] Awesome!
Where did they put it?

00:08:02.167 --> 00:08:04.855
Alright, I'm through
the neck bone now, dude!

00:08:04.855 --> 00:08:08.164
- Get over here man!
- I'm getting it!

00:08:08.164 --> 00:08:10.347
(laughter and chattering)

00:08:10.347 --> 00:08:11.180
- I got it!

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:14.344
- Alright! (laughs)

00:08:14.344 --> 00:08:15.614
- Oh!

00:08:15.614 --> 00:08:16.863
- Oh no.

00:08:16.863 --> 00:08:17.696
- Get off!

00:08:17.696 --> 00:08:19.500
- Chill out.

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:21.359
Wait, wait, okay. Wait for it!

00:08:21.359 --> 00:08:22.775
- Oh no, what are they doing?

00:08:22.775 --> 00:08:24.036
Oh no.

00:08:24.036 --> 00:08:25.683
Oh my god!

00:08:25.683 --> 00:08:26.842
Oh no.

00:08:26.842 --> 00:08:28.609
Alright, this is a perfect example of

00:08:28.609 --> 00:08:32.035
sometimes the evidence
just speaks for itself.

00:08:32.035 --> 00:08:35.050
In Latin, that's the
phrase "res ipsa loquitur",

00:08:35.050 --> 00:08:36.774
"the thing speaks for itself."

00:08:36.774 --> 00:08:39.324
And, frankly, sometimes it doesn't require

00:08:39.324 --> 00:08:40.876
any legal argument.

00:08:40.876 --> 00:08:44.628
You just show the thing
itself, and that's bad enough.

00:08:44.628 --> 00:08:46.881
Sometimes you don't need to gild the lily,

00:08:46.881 --> 00:08:50.846
or pound the rubble, it's
just bad enough as it is.

00:08:50.846 --> 00:08:53.590
And if I was the prosecutor
in this particular case,

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:55.417
I probably would've done the same thing.

00:08:55.417 --> 00:08:58.776
Just show the tape, let
it speak for itself,

00:08:58.776 --> 00:09:00.179
and let the jury decide.

00:09:00.179 --> 00:09:03.197
This doesn't need any argument at all.

00:09:03.197 --> 00:09:04.537
This is-

00:09:04.537 --> 00:09:05.565
This is awful

00:09:05.565 --> 00:09:07.438
And hilarious.

00:09:07.438 --> 00:09:09.379
- [Man] I will if you will, loser.

00:09:09.379 --> 00:09:10.462
- Alright, alright!

00:09:10.462 --> 00:09:11.848
(laughs)

00:09:11.848 --> 00:09:13.329
- The hell kind of math is that?

00:09:13.329 --> 00:09:14.726
- [Woman] $700 per defense.

00:09:14.726 --> 00:09:17.348
- No, no, no, defendant, "dant."

00:09:17.348 --> 00:09:19.065
Three defendants, $2100.

00:09:19.065 --> 00:09:21.667
Which, by the way, bargain,
what I did for them.

00:09:21.667 --> 00:09:24.114
- [Woman] They going to jail, ain't they?

00:09:24.114 --> 00:09:26.302
- So, since when does that matter?

00:09:26.302 --> 00:09:28.134
They had sex with a head!

00:09:28.134 --> 00:09:31.326
- (laughs) So I guess in this case,

00:09:31.326 --> 00:09:33.980
Saul is a public
defender. As you may know,

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:37.385
there are two types of government
attorneys in this context.

00:09:37.385 --> 00:09:40.383
There are the prosecutors,
who are employed by the state

00:09:40.383 --> 00:09:43.739
to actually prosecute
those alleged of crimes.

00:09:43.739 --> 00:09:45.512
And then there are also public defenders,

00:09:45.512 --> 00:09:48.550
who provide a defense to
those who can't afford one.

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:51.509
It goes back to the 1960's,
to a Supreme Court case

00:09:51.509 --> 00:09:53.258
called Gideon versus Wainwright,

00:09:53.258 --> 00:09:56.166
which held that under the
Sixth Amendment's protection

00:09:56.166 --> 00:10:00.216
to a trial, the defendants were
required to have an attorney

00:10:00.216 --> 00:10:02.264
because if they didn't have an attorney,

00:10:02.264 --> 00:10:06.512
the right to an impartial
trial would never be upheld

00:10:06.512 --> 00:10:09.394
because it would be inherently unfair.

00:10:09.394 --> 00:10:12.193
So, under Supreme Court
precedent, if you cannot

00:10:12.193 --> 00:10:15.228
afford an attorney, you are
provided a public defender.

00:10:15.228 --> 00:10:17.803
Now that only applies
in the criminal context,

00:10:17.803 --> 00:10:19.962
it does not apply in the civil context.

00:10:19.962 --> 00:10:23.489
If you are sued civilly,
you're basically on your own,

00:10:23.489 --> 00:10:25.572
and that's where attorneys like me,

00:10:25.572 --> 00:10:29.644
a civil attorney, come in
and we save your bacon.

00:10:29.644 --> 00:10:34.528
- You can tell me what this
$26,000 is supposed to be for.

00:10:34.528 --> 00:10:38.063
- That's money for Chuck.
Isn't that what you wanted?

00:10:38.063 --> 00:10:40.827
- A measly 26 grand? (scoffs)

00:10:40.827 --> 00:10:42.847
Jesus, you're like Peter
Minuit with the Indians.

00:10:42.847 --> 00:10:45.319
Throw in some beads and
shells while you're at it.

00:10:45.319 --> 00:10:46.643
- It's just a start, there'll be more.

00:10:46.643 --> 00:10:49.194
Unless, you're gonna
just tear them all up.

00:10:49.194 --> 00:10:51.549
- Why was it made out
to me? Why not Chuck?

00:10:51.549 --> 00:10:54.425
- So he personally told
you that it's his wish

00:10:54.425 --> 00:10:56.758
to withdrawal from the firm?

00:10:59.077 --> 00:11:01.262
See, that would surprise me.

00:11:01.262 --> 00:11:03.417
- [Jimmy] It's been nearly a
year since he set foot in here.

00:11:03.417 --> 00:11:06.122
- Okay, so one of the
reasons that Jimmy may not

00:11:06.122 --> 00:11:09.762
want to cash this check, or
have his client cash this check,

00:11:09.762 --> 00:11:12.529
is that there is a doctrine
in most jurisdictions

00:11:12.529 --> 00:11:15.992
that says that even if you
don't have a written contract,

00:11:15.992 --> 00:11:18.374
an express contract that is spelled out

00:11:18.374 --> 00:11:20.569
and has all the terms, you can sometimes

00:11:20.569 --> 00:11:23.898
enter into a contract by your conduct.

00:11:23.898 --> 00:11:25.751
So I think he's worried
about the idea that

00:11:25.751 --> 00:11:29.258
if he cashes this check,
he may tacitly agree

00:11:29.258 --> 00:11:32.210
to the terms of this settlement
with this other law firm,

00:11:32.210 --> 00:11:35.245
and therefore give them
the deal that they want,

00:11:35.245 --> 00:11:37.813
which is not a deal that
Jimmy or his client want.

00:11:37.813 --> 00:11:41.390
So I think he is worried about
forming a settlement contract

00:11:41.390 --> 00:11:42.843
by his own conduct.

00:11:42.843 --> 00:11:45.059
But, I guess we'll see.

00:11:45.059 --> 00:11:48.265
- 630 bucks. Is that for one fall?

00:11:48.265 --> 00:11:49.098
- Two.

00:11:49.098 --> 00:11:51.786
- Two falls in one day? (laughs)

00:11:51.786 --> 00:11:53.648
Even at your age, that's gotta hurt.

00:11:53.648 --> 00:11:54.648
- True that.

00:11:55.755 --> 00:11:59.027
- Right, well, I got a job for you.

00:11:59.027 --> 00:12:00.628
How's 2 grand sound?

00:12:00.628 --> 00:12:03.241
- 2 grand, for one hit?

00:12:03.241 --> 00:12:04.074
- One hit.

00:12:05.171 --> 00:12:07.413
Plus you get to learn from the best.

00:12:07.413 --> 00:12:09.717
- Okay, I probably don't need to tell you

00:12:09.717 --> 00:12:14.035
how unethical it is for an
attorney to go out and pay people

00:12:14.035 --> 00:12:18.828
to fake an injury and basically
defraud some poor victim.

00:12:18.828 --> 00:12:20.869
But let's talk about
some of the nuances here.

00:12:20.869 --> 00:12:23.475
If they were to go forward with this plan,

00:12:23.475 --> 00:12:26.818
they would be engaging in
fraud and would be liable

00:12:26.818 --> 00:12:29.649
for civil and potentially criminal fraud.

00:12:29.649 --> 00:12:32.712
Now on top of that, they
would not be able to

00:12:32.712 --> 00:12:35.785
get compensation for whatever
injuries that they have.

00:12:35.785 --> 00:12:38.486
Of course, fraud creates
an affirmative defense

00:12:38.486 --> 00:12:41.857
to the kind of allegations
that they would want to create.

00:12:41.857 --> 00:12:44.974
But more fundamentally, they
lack what's called "standing."

00:12:44.974 --> 00:12:48.958
In the US system, in order to
go forward with a civil suit,

00:12:48.958 --> 00:12:51.287
you have to have
standing. You have to show

00:12:51.287 --> 00:12:53.922
that you have suffered
an injury, and when you

00:12:53.922 --> 00:12:57.074
intentionally engage in
this kind of conduct,

00:12:57.074 --> 00:12:59.916
you negate the kind of
standing that would give rise

00:12:59.916 --> 00:13:00.994
to an injury.

00:13:00.994 --> 00:13:04.088
So, not only will they not
be able to get compensation

00:13:04.088 --> 00:13:06.124
because they have defrauded someone,

00:13:06.124 --> 00:13:08.948
but they also lack the underlying elements

00:13:08.948 --> 00:13:10.748
of the civil suit.

00:13:10.748 --> 00:13:13.341
They lack standing and they
lack an actionable injury.

00:13:13.341 --> 00:13:16.191
So if they ever get caught, this is not

00:13:16.191 --> 00:13:17.386
a good thing for them.

00:13:17.386 --> 00:13:19.136
- [Bailiff] All rise.

00:13:20.227 --> 00:13:23.163
- Okay, that was the first
episode of Better Call Saul,

00:13:23.163 --> 00:13:24.753
and it did not disappoint.

00:13:24.753 --> 00:13:27.716
I really look forward to
reviewing future episodes

00:13:27.716 --> 00:13:29.685
of this series, it's great.

00:13:29.685 --> 00:13:31.968
But now it's time to give Better Call Saul

00:13:31.968 --> 00:13:34.404
a grade for legal realism,
so let's think about this.

00:13:34.404 --> 00:13:37.618
Well, we have realistic
depictions of the monotony

00:13:37.618 --> 00:13:39.151
of courtroom life.

00:13:39.151 --> 00:13:41.907
We have some really good
arguments being made,

00:13:41.907 --> 00:13:44.722
trying to appeal to
jury's emotional state.

00:13:44.722 --> 00:13:47.051
We have big firm attorneys being jerks.

00:13:47.051 --> 00:13:49.152
We have the unglamorous
life of an attorney

00:13:49.152 --> 00:13:51.482
who is just trying to make ends meet.

00:13:51.482 --> 00:13:53.690
And we have literal
ambulance chasers who are

00:13:53.690 --> 00:13:56.229
throwing themselves in front of cars.

00:13:56.229 --> 00:13:58.803
All in all, we have a
realistic depiction of a lot

00:13:58.803 --> 00:14:01.009
of the parts that go
into a lawyer's life that

00:14:01.009 --> 00:14:02.669
rarely get any screen time,

00:14:02.669 --> 00:14:05.194
with only minimal dramatic license.

00:14:05.194 --> 00:14:08.837
So, I'm giving Better Call
Saul episode one an A-

00:14:08.837 --> 00:14:11.390
for legal realism. Well done!

00:14:11.390 --> 00:14:13.988
So hit that bell to be
notified of my next reaction,

00:14:13.988 --> 00:14:15.567
and check out this playlist I put together

00:14:15.567 --> 00:14:17.301
that includes all of my prior reactions,

00:14:17.301 --> 00:14:20.019
including my reaction
to Suits, The Good Wife,

00:14:20.019 --> 00:14:21.244
and many others.

00:14:21.244 --> 00:14:23.894
So click on that playlist,
and I'll see you in court!

